{"name":"Deserted Island","hex":"#857c64","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"deserted","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of desert"]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Abandoned, without people.","desolate (Can we add an example for this sense?)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/deserted"},{"term":"island","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A contiguous area of land, smaller than a continent, totally surrounded by water.","A contiguous area of land, smaller than a continent, partially surrounded by water; a peninsula; a half-island."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To surround with water; make into an island.","To set, dot (as if) with islands."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/island"}],"entries":[{"title":"Uninhabited island","blocks":[{"text":"An uninhabited island, desert island, or deserted island, is an island, islet, or atoll which lacks permanent human population. Uninhabited islands are often depicted in films or stories about shipwrecked people, and are also used as stereotypes for the idea of \"paradise\". Some uninhabited islands are protected as nature reserves, and some are privately owned. Devon Island in Canada's far north is the largest uninhabited island in the world."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Desertisland.jpg/330px-Desertisland.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uninhabited_island"},{"title":"Island","blocks":[{"text":"An island or isle is a piece of land, distinct from a continent, completely surrounded by water. There are continental islands, which were formed by being split from a continent by plate tectonics, and oceanic islands, which have never been part of a continent. Oceanic islands can be formed from volcanic activity, grow into atolls from coral reefs, and form from sediment along shorelines, creating barrier islands. River islands can also form from sediment and debris in rivers. Artificial islands are those made by humans, including small rocky outcroppings built out of lagoons and large-scale land reclamation projects used for development."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Hawaje-NoRedLine.jpg/330px-Hawaje-NoRedLine.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Island"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/LighthouseOnKleinCuracao.jpg/500px-LighthouseOnKleinCuracao.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/LighthouseOnKleinCuracao.jpg/500px-LighthouseOnKleinCuracao.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The abandoned lighthouse at Klein Curaçao","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALighthouseOnKleinCuracao.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Baa_atoll_islands.JPG/330px-Baa_atoll_islands.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Baa_atoll_islands.JPG/960px-Baa_atoll_islands.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Baa_atoll_islands.JPG/330px-Baa_atoll_islands.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABaa_atoll_islands.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Maldives_island.JPG/330px-Maldives_island.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Maldives_island.JPG/960px-Maldives_island.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=